<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Nyungu ya Mawe's state</h1><p class="st-meta">1870 CE – 1884 CE (approx.) · capital Kiwele</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived conquest state in central Tanzania, built by the Nyamwezi warlord Nyungu ya Mawe with ruga-ruga gunmen.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyungu-ya-mawe">Nyungu ya Mawe</a>, a member of the ruling family of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/unyanyembe">Unyanyembe</a>, broke with his kin and the Arab traders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabora">Tabora</a> in the 1860s. From around 1870 he led bands of ruga-ruga to conquer the chiefdoms of Ukimbu, south of Tabora, and established his base at Kiwele.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Unlike most Nyamwezi chiefs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyungu-ya-mawe">Nyungu</a> ruled through appointed officials, the vatwale, rather than hereditary chiefs, creating an unusually centralised state. His power rested on firearms and control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> to the south, which he raided or taxed. He controlled a large area of Ukimbu until his death.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyungu-ya-mawe">Nyungu ya Mawe</a> died in 1884. His daughter Mgalula held the state together for some years, and it was absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a> in the 1890s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1870 CE – 1884 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Horn and East Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.92%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1870 CE – 1884 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kiwele</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nyungu ya Mawe</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nyamwezi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/tanzania">Tanzania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kiwele</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1877&amp;focus=nyungu-ya-mawes-state" aria-label="Nyungu ya Mawe's state on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5645 2417 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nyungu ya Mawe's state lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5965 2673h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5965 2673h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Tanzania. <a href="/atlas?year=1877&amp;focus=nyungu-ya-mawes-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

